\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces  \centering  Before \& After: Lee filter Application  This figure visualizes the effect of applying a Lee filter to a SAR image—a key preprocessing step in ship and dark vessel detection. The left panel (“Original”) shows the raw SAR imagery, where ships and coastal features are visible but surrounded by significant speckle noise. The right panel (“After Lee Filter”) demonstrates how the Lee filter smooths the sea surface, effectively reducing noise while preserving the major coastline and vessel signatures.}}{21}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:lee_filter_comparison}{{5}{21}{\centering Before \& After: Lee filter Application\\ This figure visualizes the effect of applying a Lee filter to a SAR image—a key preprocessing step in ship and dark vessel detection. The left panel (“Original”) shows the raw SAR imagery, where ships and coastal features are visible but surrounded by significant speckle noise. The right panel (“After Lee Filter”) demonstrates how the Lee filter smooths the sea surface, effectively reducing noise while preserving the major coastline and vessel signatures}{figure.caption.16}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \centering  Before \& After: Multi-stage Denoising \newline  This figure compares SAR image patches before and after multi-stage denoising and enhancement—a crucial step in the preprocessing pipeline for ship and dark vessel detection. The left image ("Before Enhancement") retains some noise and less pronounced vessel and coastline features. The right image ("After Enhancement") shows clearer separation between land, sea, and detected objects, with reduced noise and more distinct ship-like targets.}}{22}{figure.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:denoising_comparison}{{6}{22}{\centering Before \& After: Multi-stage Denoising \newline This figure compares SAR image patches before and after multi-stage denoising and enhancement—a crucial step in the preprocessing pipeline for ship and dark vessel detection. The left image ("Before Enhancement") retains some noise and less pronounced vessel and coastline features. The right image ("After Enhancement") shows clearer separation between land, sea, and detected objects, with reduced noise and more distinct ship-like targets}{figure.caption.17}{}}
\newlabel{fig:threshold_comparison}{{7a}{22}{Application of Thresholds}{figure.caption.18}{}}
\newlabel{sub@fig:threshold_comparison}{{a}{22}{Application of Thresholds}{figure.caption.18}{}}
\newlabel{fig:morph_comparison}{{7b}{22}{Before \& After: Morphological Processing}{figure.caption.18}{}}
\newlabel{sub@fig:morph_comparison}{{b}{22}{Before \& After: Morphological Processing}{figure.caption.18}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \centering  Comparison of image processing techniques  The left subpanel shows the results of Otsu thresholding (global method) and adaptive thresholding (local method). These techniques convert grayscale SAR imagery into binary land–and–sea masks by isolating regions with distinct radar backscatter. Accurate segmentation is fundamental for excluding land from detection processes—ensuring ships and dark vessels in the water are not confused with terrestrial features.  The right subpanel illustrates how morphological operations (opening and closing) refine the binary mask produced by thresholding. Before morphology, small gaps and noisy areas remain in the land mask, potentially leading to detection errors. After morphology, the mask is smoothed and small artifacts are removed, resulting in more precise separation between sea and land. }}{22}{figure.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:comparison}{{7}{22}{\centering Comparison of image processing techniques\\ The left subpanel shows the results of Otsu thresholding (global method) and adaptive thresholding (local method). These techniques convert grayscale SAR imagery into binary land–and–sea masks by isolating regions with distinct radar backscatter. Accurate segmentation is fundamental for excluding land from detection processes—ensuring ships and dark vessels in the water are not confused with terrestrial features.\\ The right subpanel illustrates how morphological operations (opening and closing) refine the binary mask produced by thresholding. Before morphology, small gaps and noisy areas remain in the land mask, potentially leading to detection errors. After morphology, the mask is smoothed and small artifacts are removed, resulting in more precise separation between sea and land}{figure.caption.18}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Noise Reduction and edge enhancing}{22}{section*.21}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces \centering  Final Mask Obtained   This figure shows the final land mask generated during SAR image preprocessing. The mask delineates land (white) from sea (black), effectively excluding terrestrial regions from subsequent ship detection analysis.}}{23}{figure.caption.19}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:final_mask}{{8}{23}{\centering Final Mask Obtained \\ This figure shows the final land mask generated during SAR image preprocessing. The mask delineates land (white) from sea (black), effectively excluding terrestrial regions from subsequent ship detection analysis}{figure.caption.19}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces \centering  Comparization between Original Image and Resuts of first and second preprocessing iteration  This figure shows that land masking is applied repeatedly throughout the preprocessing pipeline to fully eliminate land areas from SAR images. The sequence demonstrates how each preprocessing iteration further refines the segmentation mask—progressively removing residual land regions and isolating only the sea.}}{23}{figure.caption.20}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:masking_iteration_comparison}{{9}{23}{\centering Comparization between Original Image and Resuts of first and second preprocessing iteration\\ This figure shows that land masking is applied repeatedly throughout the preprocessing pipeline to fully eliminate land areas from SAR images. The sequence demonstrates how each preprocessing iteration further refines the segmentation mask—progressively removing residual land regions and isolating only the sea}{figure.caption.20}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces  \centering  Before \& After: Noise Reduction \& Edge Enhancement  The figure displays a side-by-side comparison of a SAR (Synthetic Aperture Radar) image before and after noise reduction and edge enhancement. The left image ("Before filter") shows prominent speckle noise and less distinct ship targets, while the right image ("After filter") demonstrates clearer, enhanced ship signatures against a smoother background.}}{23}{figure.caption.22}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:noise_reduction_comparison}{{10}{23}{\centering Before \& After: Noise Reduction \& Edge Enhancement\\ The figure displays a side-by-side comparison of a SAR (Synthetic Aperture Radar) image before and after noise reduction and edge enhancement. The left image ("Before filter") shows prominent speckle noise and less distinct ship targets, while the right image ("After filter") demonstrates clearer, enhanced ship signatures against a smoother background}{figure.caption.22}{}}
